# 6. Core Counseling Attributes (32).
CAT = "ncmhceAttributes"; SEG = "ATT"
Q = [
("A client says, \"My sister skipped my wedding without a word.\" The most empathic response is:",
 "\"That really stung.\"", ["\"Sisters can be like that.\"", "\"What did you do to her?\"", "\"You should call her today.\""],
 "An empathic response names the feeling beneath the content. Excusing the sister, blaming the client or advising skips her experience."),
("A client asks, \"Do you even like me?\" after the counselor seemed distracted. A genuine response is:",
 "\"I was distracted. Sorry.\"", ["\"Of course I like everyone.\"", "\"Why do you ask that?\"", "\"Let's stay on topic now.\""],
 "Genuineness means being honest and real. Owning the lapse repairs trust; a blanket reassurance sounds hollow."),
("A counselor sets aside her own reactions to a client's story so she can see it as he does. This is:",
 "Bracketing", ["Projection", "Transference", "Intellectualizing"],
 "Bracketing, or reflexivity, means noticing and setting aside one's own assumptions and feelings so they do not color the client's story."),
("A client snaps, \"This is a waste of my time.\" The best response is:",
 "\"You're fed up with this.\"", ["\"Then why are you here?\"", "\"Therapy takes time.\"", "\"Let's try something else.\""],
 "An empathic reflection of the frustration keeps the alliance and invites more. Defending therapy or changing course at once skips his feeling."),
("A transgender client is misgendered by the intake form. The counselor should:",
 "Use her name and pronouns", ["Use the legal name on file", "Avoid pronouns entirely", "Wait for her to raise it first"],
 "Knowledge of and sensitivity to gender identity includes asking about and using the client's name and pronouns, and fixing forms that misgender."),
("A counselor notices she assumes a Muslim client's wife has no say in decisions. The counselor should:",
 "Ask the client", ["Act on the assumption", "Refer the client out", "Avoid cultural topics"],
 "Multicultural competence starts with awareness of one's own assumptions. Asking the client avoids stereotyping."),
("A client of a different race says, \"You probably can't understand me.\" A culturally responsive reply is:",
 "\"Tell me what I might miss.\"", ["\"Race doesn't matter here.\"", "\"I treat everyone the same.\"", "\"I have friends like you.\""],
 "Inviting the client to name what the counselor may not understand respects her experience. Color-blind replies dismiss it."),
("A client from a culture where steady eye contact with elders is rude avoids the counselor's gaze. The counselor should:",
 "Not read it as avoidance", ["Note it as social anxiety", "Ask her to look at him", "Record poor rapport"],
 "Attending behaviors and nonverbal cues vary by culture. Reading gaze through the counselor's own norms risks a wrong conclusion about the client."),
("A counselor wants to confirm a single fact quickly. Which type of question fits best?",
 "A closed question", ["An open question", "A leading question", "A double question"],
 "Closed questions are useful for specific facts, such as dates or doses. Open questions invite exploration; leading and double questions confuse or steer the client."),
("A counselor says \"Mm-hmm\" and \"Go on\" as a client talks. These are:",
 "Minimal encouragers", ["Interpretations", "Brief summaries", "Gentle confrontations"],
 "Minimal encouragers are brief verbal and nonverbal prompts that keep the client talking without directing the content."),
("A client says, \"I moved, lost my job and my dog died, all in a month.\" A paraphrase is:",
 "\"So much changed at once.\"", ["\"Why did you move?\"", "\"Dogs are good company.\"", "\"You'll find another job.\""],
 "Paraphrasing restates the essence of what the client said in fresh words, showing understanding without adding advice."),
("A client says prayer helps her cope. The counselor, who is not religious, should:",
 "Explore prayer as a strength", ["Suggest a secular option", "Avoid the subject", "Share her own doubts"],
 "The outline lists exploring religious and spiritual values. Treating the client's faith as a resource respects her values; steering her away imposes the counselor's."),
("A client is angry at the counselor for raising the fee and voices it in session. The counselor should:",
 "Talk it through", ["End the session early", "Waive the fee to calm her", "Defend the fee at length"],
 "Tolerating and working through conflict in session models healthy repair. Avoiding it or giving in teaches neither party anything."),
("A counselor whose mother died of cancer has a client grieving the same loss. The counselor should:",
 "Monitor her own reactions", ["Share her story often", "Refer the client at once", "Push the client's grief faster"],
 "Awareness of self and impact on clients means noticing personal reactions so they do not steer the session, and using supervision as needed."),
("A client says, \"I don't know why I'm crying.\" The counselor stays silent for a few moments. This:",
 "Gives room to feel", ["Shows lack of interest", "Ends the topic", "Signals disapproval"],
 "Brief, attentive silence lets the client stay with the feeling and often leads to deeper reflection."),
("A client describes her mood as \"heavy and gray.\" The counselor replies, \"Heavy, like it weighs you down.\" This is:",
 "Empathic attunement", ["Interpretation", "Gentle confrontation", "Advice giving"],
 "Staying close to the client's own words and images shows attunement to her inner experience."),
("An older client says young counselors cannot help him. The counselor should:",
 "Invite him to say more", ["List her credentials", "Agree and refer him out", "Change the subject"],
 "Exploring the concern without defensiveness respects the client and often strengthens the alliance."),
("A counselor calls a blind client \"so inspiring\" for holding a job, and the client frowns. The remark likely felt:",
 "Patronizing", ["Encouraging", "Neutral", "Validating"],
 "Praising ordinary achievements as extraordinary because of a disability is patronizing. Respect for diversity means treating the client as a capable adult."),
("A group member opens up for the first time and others thank her. The leader names this support. This fosters:",
 "Therapeutic factors", ["Dependence on the leader", "Subgrouping by age", "Scapegoating of one member"],
 "Fostering the emergence of group therapeutic factors, such as cohesion and acceptance, is a listed core attribute for group leaders."),
("A client says she volunteers because her late mother taught her to give back. The counselor says, \"Serving others keeps you close to her.\" This is:",
 "Reflection of meaning", ["Paraphrase of content", "Interpretation of a defense", "Open questioning"],
 "Reflecting meaning names the values or significance behind what the client says, here her bond with her mother, going beyond the facts of volunteering."),
("A client says she believes her counselor judges her drinking. The counselor feels defensive. The best first step is to:",
 "Reflect her concern", ["Prove she is not judging", "Change the topic quickly", "End the session early"],
 "Managing her own reaction and reflecting the client's concern repairs the rupture and keeps the client talking."),
("A counselor uses the same warm tone with every client but ignores cultural cues. This most risks:",
 "Missing the context", ["Too much empathy", "Breaking confidentiality", "Billing errors"],
 "Effective counselors adapt to culture; treating everyone the same can overlook how culture shapes the client's experience."),
("A client says her brother died last month and she feels \"nothing.\" The counselor says, \"Feeling numb can be part of shock.\" This response:",
 "Normalizes it", ["Diagnoses a disorder", "Minimizes the loss", "Challenges her denial"],
 "Normalizing tells the client a reaction is common, which reduces shame and opens the way to talk."),
("A gay client fears his counselor will disapprove of him. The counselor's best stance is:",
 "Affirming and accepting", ["Neutral and silent", "Curious about his choice", "Focused on changing it"],
 "Affirming, accepting care is the ethical standard. Treating orientation as a choice to change is harmful and against ethics codes."),
("A counselor nods but is planning her next question while the client talks. She is missing:",
 "Active listening", ["Confrontation", "Positive regard", "Self-disclosure skills"],
 "Active listening means full attention to the client's words and feelings, not preparing the next response."),
("A client says the counselor's remark that her English is \"so good\" felt hurtful. The counselor should:",
 "Own it and apologize", ["Explain what she meant", "Say it was a compliment", "Change the subject"],
 "A comment that implies a client is foreign is a microaggression. Owning its impact and apologizing repairs the alliance; defending intent dismisses her experience."),
("A counselor feels sexually attracted to a client. The ethical course is to:",
 "Seek supervision", ["Tell the client", "Act on it after therapy", "Ignore it completely"],
 "Attraction is a human reaction; acting on it or disclosing it harms the client. Supervision helps the counselor manage it."),
("A client says, \"I keep pushing people away.\" The counselor replies, \"Part of you wants closeness and part fears it.\" This reflects:",
 "Ambivalence", ["Resistance", "Denial of need", "Projection"],
 "Reflecting both sides of mixed feelings helps the client explore them. It is not resistance to treatment."),
("A client says she is going to leave counseling because it is not helping. The counselor should:",
 "Ask what has not helped", ["Agree and close the file", "List her progress", "Warn her of relapse"],
 "Asking for feedback without defensiveness shows genuineness and can rescue the alliance or improve the plan."),
("A counselor's facial expression tightens as a client describes her sexual history, and the client stops talking. The counselor should:",
 "Notice her own reaction", ["Change the subject", "Ask why she stopped", "Refer the client out"],
 "The counselor's nonverbal reactions affect clients. Self-awareness lets her repair the moment and stay open."),
("A client says, \"Nobody understands me.\" Which response shows deeper empathy?",
 "\"You feel very alone.\"", ["\"I understand you.\"", "\"Everyone feels that.\"", "\"Have you told people?\""],
 "Reflecting the feeling behind the words goes deeper than claiming to understand or normalizing it away."),
("A client says, \"I'm doing great!\" while describing losing her job. The counselor gently names the mixed message. This shows:",
 "Caring feedback", ["Harsh judgment", "Lack of empathy", "Disinterest in her"],
 "Genuine counselors offer honest observations in a caring way, which helps the client look at what she may be avoiding."),
]
